provides testing utilities—but I'll need to reference the actual PRD documents in my required reading since the project context doesn't include their full contents.\n\nSince both are libraries that other packages depend on, I need to pick which one to implement first. pkg/testutils/ seems more foundational as a differential testing harness that would support the entire verification pipeline, so that's probably the right choice to prioritize. libraries before consumers.\" Both are libraries. \"Break ties by primary file path alphabetically.\" pkg/sys/ comes before pkg/testutils/ alphabetically.\n\nHowever, I also need to check what's most needed. The architecture says pkg/sys/ provides terminal queries, signal handling, file metadata, etc. The pkg/format/color.go already has a local isTerminal() function with a comment saying \"when pkg/sys is available on this branch, this should delegate to pkg/sys.IsTerminal per prd003-format R2.3.\"\n\nSo pkg/sys/ is a dependency of pkg/format/ (at least for isTerminal). And pkg/testutils/ is needed for all differential tests.\n\nGiven I can only propose 1 task, and alphabetical ordering gives pkg/sys/ priority, let me propose pkg/sys/.\n\nBut wait - I need to be careful about task sizing. Looking at the capabilities listed for pkg/sys/, there's terminal size queries, signal handling, file metadata, process group management, and process priority. That's potentially a lot to fit within the 150-250 line limit with max 4 requirements. I need to scope this carefully and find the actual PRD to understand what's in scope for this task. I'm realizing I need to work with the architecture details provided rather than trying to access PRD files I don't have. Since pkg/sys/ needs to support terminal detection for pkg/format/, I should focus the requirements around that core capability along with the other system-level operations the architecture describes—signal handling, file metadata, and process management—keeping it to four requirements