ments, while still outlining what I understand the task needs to accomplish based on the architecture.\n\nThe architecture describes pkg/sys as wrapping Darwin and Linux syscalls for terminal detection and file metadata, so I should frame the task around those core responsibilities rather than guessing at specific requirement IDs. I'm narrowing down to three core capabilities that fit the 150-250 line budget: checking if a file descriptor is a terminal, getting terminal dimensions, and wrapping file metadata with consistent field names across platforms. Signal handling might be too much scope, so I'll focus on these three as the requirements and structure the task around the primary exported types like `terminal.go` and `fileinfo.go` following the naming convention.\n\nLooking at the existing code in `pkg/format/color.go`, I can see there's already a local implementation of terminal detection using syscalls that should eventually delegate to `pkg/sys` once it's available. I need to make sure my task captures this dependency and provides the abstraction that the format package is expecting.\n\nNow I'm weighing whether to prioritize `pkg/sys` or `pkg/testutils` as my single task—`pkg/testutils` is foundational for all differential testing and verification, but `pkg/sys` is the immediate blocker for the format package's refactoring goals. I'm narrowing the scope to the most critical capabilities for the initial release: terminal detection and size reporting via ioctl, plus unified file metadata access that abstracts away platform differences in timestamp and block count fields. Signal handling and process group management can wait for later utilities, so I'll target three to four requirements that fit within the 150-200 line range. I'm handling the platform divergence in stat fields—Darwin uses st_mtimespec while Linux uses st_mtim—and abstracting block counts for the du command, which gives me four solid requirements across roughly 150-200 lines. I'll organize this into separate files for terminal logic, a shared fileinfo wrapper, and platform-specific implementations for Darwin and Linux that leverage golang.org/x/sys for the underlying Stat_t field access.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the naming convention—since pkg/sys exports functions rather than a primary type, I should group by concern with terminal.go for IsTerminal and TerminalSize, then stat.go for the file metadata abstraction, with stat_darwin.go and stat_linux.go handling platform-specific details. This keeps the implementation focused, and tests can come as a separate task afterward.\n\nFor the task definition, I need to reference the PRD and use case documents along with the architecture spec, letting the stitch agent resolve the actual file paths.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific files to create: terminal functions using ioctl calls for detecting terminal capabilities and getting window size, platform-independent stat abstractions, and OS-specific implementations for Darwin and Linux to handle their different struct field naming conventions. I'll use build tags to handle the platform differences between Darwin and Linux when accessing syscall fields, and rely on golang.org/x/sys/unix for the TIOCGWINSZ constant and cross-platform Stat_t definitions. The architecture keeps cmd/ packages isolated from external dependencies by importing through pkg/sys, so I'll organize the terminal functions in terminal.go and split the file stat abstraction across stat.go with platform-specific implementations in stat_darwin.go and stat_linux.go.\n\nNow I'm laying out the acceptance criteria—the terminal and stat packages need to export the right functions, compile cleanly on both platforms, and pass go vet checks. Let me double-check the PRD file naming convention to make sure I'm using the right format. I need to reconsider the numbering scheme — PRDs are numbered globally while use cases are numbered per release, so I don't actually know which PRD number corresponds to sys. I'll make the required_reading reference more flexible by using a glob pattern hint so the stitch agent can find the right file. Now looking more closely at the format source code, particularly how isTerminal is implemented in color.go...\n\nThe current implementation uses raw syscalls rather than golang.org/x/sys, so it already works on the current platform, but creating a pkg/sys abstraction would be cleaner. For sizing, I'm estimating terminal.go at around 30-40 lines, stat.go at 40-50 lines, and the platform-specific implementations at 30-40 lines each, putting the total somewhere between 130-170 lines. I should check if there's a test suite document that might give me more context about what's expected.\n\nFor the file naming, since pkg/sys doesn't have a single primary exported type but rather functions grouped by concern, naming files like terminal.go and stat.go makes sense. I also need to verify that go.mod already includes golang.org/x/sys as a dependency since the project lists it in its technology choices, and the module path should be github.com/pe...\n\nActually, I should double-check whether go.mod needs updating and whether running `go mod tidy` would be necessary. My design decisions are all grounded in the architecture—D1 from the technology choices, D2 from the constraint about cmd/ packages not importing golang.org/x/sys directly, and D3 from the file organization principles in the architecture.\n\nI'm realizing I don't have the specific PRD requirement IDs for the sys package, which the constraint requires me to anchor to. The architecture mentions that shared library components like pkg/sys may have their own PRDs, and I can see from the prd003-format references in the codebase that format has requirements like R1.1, R1.2, etc., but I'd need to know the equivalent IDs for sys to properly structure the tasks.\n\nSince I don't have access to those specific requirement IDs, I'll use the use case ID in the task title instead: \"pkg/sys -- terminal and file stat abstraction (rel00.0-uc003-sys)\". I should probably check if there's actually a PRD file in the docs/specs/product-requirements/ directory that I'm missing, but I think I'm overthinking this at this point.\n\nNow I'm weighing whether to tackle pkg/sys/ or pkg/testutils/ first. pkg/testutils/ is foundational for the entire verification methodology and would unblock all the cmd/ work, but pkg/sys/ comes first alphabetically and pkg/format/ is already waiting for it. The alphabetical tiebreaker settles it—pkg/sys/ it is.\n\nHere's the task: create the pkg/sys package with terminal and file stat abstractions, starting with the terminal.go file that implements IsTerminal and Term functionality. I need to reference the PRD for sys, the use case spec, the architecture docs, and ensure pkg/format/color.go delegates to this new package per the requirements.\n\nNow I'm laying out the remaining files: stat.go for the platform-independent file metadata layer, then Darwin and Linux-specific implementations to handle the differences in how Stat_t exposes modification time and block count across systems. The requirements spell out three main pieces: IsTerminal using TIOCGWINSZ ioctl, TerminalSize for getting rows and columns, and a file metadata abstraction with build-tagged platform support. I need to anchor my implementation to the specific requirement IDs in the PRD, using exact struct names and function signatures as written. The acceptance criteria show I need to export IsTerminal and TerminalSize from terminal.go, provide platform-independent file stat access through stat.go with Darwin and Linux implementations, and ensure the code builds and passes vetting without errors.\n\nD3 is too prescriptive—it's telling the agent what to do rather than stating a design decision, so I should replace it. I'm also realizing the required_reading glob pattern isn't ideal, and I need to figure out the actual PRD filename. Looking at the existing pattern, prd003-format maps to pkg/format/ and prd011-magefiles to magefiles/, but the numbering seems arbitrary. Since the use case numbering suggests sys is the third use case, the PRD could be any number. Rather than guessing, I'll use a cleaner directory reference that lets the stitch agent find the right file.\n\nI should also verify whether go.mod needs updating—it doesn't require explicit package registration as long as the import path is under the module path, but I'll include it in required_reading to be safe. One more consideration: whether pkg/format/color.go should be updated to use the new pkg/sys package.\n\nIsTerminal, but that's a separate task outside the scope of just creating pkg/sys. The current task is focused solely on establishing the new package, so I'll keep the description clean without mentioning follow-up work.\n\nLooking at the remaining work for release 00.0, pkg/sys needs to come first since it's alphabetically prior and pkg/format depends on it for terminal detection capabilities. The task is to implement this package with abstractions for terminal detection, size queries, and file metadata.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":38461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37d21634-8f03-410e-9bc6-272ca24d124b","uuid":"072355ad-222e-4527-bfcf-99469c095b68"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PCTmAtZs5jsNV6qmiE29BB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge on edge cases. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Shared libraries in `pkg/` (sys, format, testutils), per-utility implementations in `cmd/`, human-authored specs in `docs/`. The generation pipeline targets only `cmd/` and `pkg/`.\n\n**Current state**: `pkg/format/` already exists with source code (color.go, column.go, size.go). All other packages show `codestatus: not started` on the working branch. Code for other packages exists only at generation-merged tags, not on HEAD.\n\n**Current release**: 00.0 (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure). Four use cases:\n- uc001-magefiles — **excluded** (DD7: magefiles/ not a generation target)\n- uc002-format — **done** (source code exists in pkg/format/)\n- uc003-sys — **needs implementation**\n- uc004-testutils — **needs implementation**\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nTwo packages remain in release 00.0: `pkg/sys/` and `pkg/testutils/`. Neither depends on the other. Per P8 alphabetical tiebreaker, `pkg/sys/` comes first. It is also a dependency of `pkg/format/` — `color.go:124` has a TODO to delegate `isTerminal` to `pkg/sys.IsTerminal`. Per P2, implementation and tests are split into separate tasks; this task covers implementation only.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"pkg/sys -- IsTerminal, TerminalSize, and file stat abstraction (rel00.0-uc003-sys)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find the PRD for pkg/sys -- file name matches prd*sys*.yaml; read fully for requirement IDs and struct/function signatures)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc003-sys.yaml (use case for sys package)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/sys component description, design decisions, technology choices section on golang.org/x/sys)\n      - pkg/format/color.go (lines 119-128 -- local isTerminal that should delegate to pkg/sys.IsTerminal per prd003-format R2.3)\n      - go.mod (verify golang.org/x/sys dependency is present)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/sys/terminal.go\n        action: create\n        note: IsTerminal and TerminalSize functions using TIOCGWINSZ ioctl via golang.org/x/sys/unix\n      - path: pkg/sys/stat.go\n        action: create\n        note: Platform-independent file stat types and accessor functions\n      - path: pkg/sys/stat_darwin.go\n        action: create\n        note: Darwin-specific Stat_t field access (Mtimespec, Blocks)\n      - path: pkg/sys/stat_linux.go\n        action: create\n        note: Linux-specific Stat_t field access (Mtim, Blocks)\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool using the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl via golang.org/x/sys/unix. Returns true only for terminal file descriptors. This function is the intended delegate for pkg/format/color.go's local isTerminal (prd003-format R2.3).\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement TerminalSize(fd uintptr) (rows int, cols int, err error) using the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl to return terminal dimensions in rows and columns.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement file metadata abstraction providing platform-independent access to modification time and disk block count from syscall.Stat_t. Use build-tagged files (stat_darwin.go, stat_linux.go) to handle field name divergence: Mtimespec on Darwin vs Mtim on Linux for modification time.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Use golang.org/x/sys/unix for ioctl constants (TIOCGWINSZ) and Stat_t field access. This is the only permitted external dependency. cmd/ packages import pkg/sys -- never golang.org/x/sys directly.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"golang.org/x/sys is the only permitted external dependency per ARCHITECTURE.yaml technology choices. All platform-specific syscall access goes through this package.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Terminal functions (IsTerminal, TerminalSize) go in terminal.go. File stat abstraction goes in stat.go with platform-specific implementations in stat_darwin.go and stat_linux.go using Go build tags.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"If the pkg/sys PRD specifies concrete struct names, function signatures, or implementation patterns, use them exactly as written. The PRD is the single source of truth for API design.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"pkg/sys/terminal.go exports IsTerminal and TerminalSize functions.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"pkg/sys/stat.go plus stat_darwin.go and stat_linux.go provide platform-independent access to file modification time and block count using build tags.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/sys/ succeeds with no errors on the current platform.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go vet ./pkg/sys/ reports no issues.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":38461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37d21634-8f03-410e-9bc6-272ca24d124b","uuid":"017ae4bd-9b67-44e7-9881-238aaec2787a"}
